--TEST--
A `spawn script` child given `grants:` holds only the capabilities named there, intersected with what its parent held: a name the parent never held stays lacking, and a child that gave one up cannot ask for it back — `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing`, `rule:security/isolate-budget-is-the-trees`, `rule:security/capability-question-is-grant-and-scope` and `rule:security/denial-is-a-runtime-error`
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`: `spawn script` goes through a
# door that asks for `script.spawn` first, and deny-by-default means a case with
# no configuration cannot spawn at all. Both grants are the parent's, and every
# refusal below is a `grants:` list taking one of them away again.
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true

[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
--FILE note.txt--
note
--FILE reader.nvs--
<?nvs
// `fs.read` is named in this child's `grants:` and held by its parent, so it
// survives the intersection and the read answers.
echo "reader read ", Core\IO::read('./note.txt');

// `script.spawn` is not named in it, though the parent holds it. A narrowing
// takes a capability away from everything beneath it, so the child is refused
// at the same door its parent passed — and refused as `rule:security/denial-is-a-runtime-error`'s
// catchable `RuntimeError` rather than as a `FATAL`.
try {
    spawn script "note.nvs" with(grants: ["fs.read"]);
    echo "unreachable", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
    echo "reader: ", $denied->message, "\n";
}
--FILE stranger.nvs--
<?nvs
// `net.connect` is a name the parent never held. `grants:` intersects with what
// the parent holds rather than replacing it, so naming it grants nothing — and
// `fs.read`, which the parent *did* hold, is gone because this list did not
// name it.
try {
    Core\IO::read('./note.txt');
    echo "unreachable", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
    echo "stranger refused", "\n";
}
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The parent's own reading first, so that what a child prints is a comparison
// against a known grant rather than an absolute.
echo "parent read ", Core\IO::read('./note.txt');

// `output: 'capture'` for the reason a-child-shares-nothing-with-its-parent
// gives: the child's lines arrive here as a value the parent prints, so their
// order against the parent's own is a fact rather than a race.
var $reader = spawn script "reader.nvs" with(grants: ["fs.read"], output: "capture");
var $read = await $reader;
echo "reader ok=", $read->ok ? "true" : "false", "\n";
echo $read->output;

var $stranger = spawn script "stranger.nvs" with(grants: ["net.connect"], output: "capture");
var $strange = await $stranger;
echo "stranger ok=", $strange->ok ? "true" : "false", "\n";
echo $strange->output;

// And the parent kept everything it narrowed for its children: a `grants:` is
// the child's list, never an edit of the list it was spawned from.
echo "parent still reads ", Core\IO::read('./note.txt');
--EXPECTF--
parent read note
reader ok=true
reader read note
reader: `spawn script` needs the capability `script.spawn` for %s%enote.nvs, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.script]`
stranger ok=true
stranger refused
parent still reads note
